The Arduino Skeleton 💀
Every Arduino program (sketch) needs two functions:
void setup() {
  // Runs ONCE when the robot turns on.
  // Used to set up pins, start serial, etc.
}

void loop() {
  // Runs FOREVER, over and over again.
  // This is where the main logic lives!
  // Note: Code runs line by line until finished, then restarts the loop from the beginning.
}

Talking to Pins 🔌
From Session 1, we learned that pins can be OUTPUT (like LEDs or Motors) or INPUT (like Sensors).
const int ledPin = 9;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600); // Start talking to computer
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH); // Turn ON (5V)
  delay(1000);                // Wait 1 sec
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);  // Turn OFF (0V)
  delay(1000);
}

Why Functions? 🛠️
In Session 2, we learned that writing the same motor code over and over makes our program messy. Function let us ‘package’ code into a single command!


Instead of this:

digitalWrite(ML_Ctrl, HIGH); 
analogWrite(ML_PWM, 150);    
digitalWrite(MR_Ctrl, HIGH); 
analogWrite(MR_PWM, 150);   

We write it ONCE:
void car_front() {
  digitalWrite(ML_Ctrl, HIGH); 
  analogWrite(ML_PWM, 150);    
  digitalWrite(MR_Ctrl, HIGH); 
  analogWrite(MR_PWM, 150); 
}


And then just call car_front(); in our loop!

Motor Setup Recap 🚗
Remember to set all motor pins as OUTPUTs!
const int MR_Ctrl = 2; const int MR_PWM = 6;
const int ML_Ctrl = 4; const int ML_PWM = 5;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ML_Ctrl, OUTPUT); pinMode(ML_PWM, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(MR_Ctrl, OUTPUT); pinMode(MR_PWM, OUTPUT);
}
Quick Quiz: Which function controls the speed?







The Answer


analogWrite controls speed from 0 to 255!

The Helper Function that Returns 🦇
In Session 3, we made a special function. It doesn’t just DO something, it gives back an answer!
int measureDistance() {
  // Send the sound wave
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  // Listen for echo and do the math
  long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);
  return duration * 0.034 / 2; // GIVES BACK the distance!
}

Sweeping the Servo 🧹
We use the &lt;Servo.h&gt; library to command our radar’s motor.
#include &lt;Servo.h&gt;
Servo myServo;

void setup() {
  myServo.attach(10); // Servo is on pin 10
}

void loop() {
  myServo.write(0);   // Look right
  delay(500);
  myServo.write(180); // Look left
  delay(500);
  myServo.write(90);  // Look forward
  delay(500);
}

If / Else Logic ⚖️
The core of Artificial Intelligence (and our robot) is making decisions based on data.
int distance = measureDistance();

if (distance &lt; 20) {
  // Danger! Path blocked!
  car_back();
  delay(500);
  car_Stop();
} else {
  // All clear!
  car_front();
}
This single block of code is what turns a remote-controlled toy into an Autonomous Explorer.

Coding Distance: Pins & Setup
const int trigPin = 12;
const int echoPin = 13;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Start serial monitor
  pinMode(trigPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(echoPin, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // We will add the logic here!
}

From Time to Distance 📐

Calculation: Divide the total time by 2 (to find distance to the object).

// Method A: Using Speed of Sound (0.034 cm/us)
int distance = duration * 0.034 / 2;

// Method B: Using Time Constant (29.1 us/cm)
int distance = (duration / 2) / 29.1; 






Note


Keyestudio documentation often uses Method B. Both give the same result!

Coding Distance: The Math
void loop() {
  // Send a 10 microsecond pulse
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  // Measure how long the Echo pin stays HIGH
  long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);

  // Calculate distance in cm (Speed of sound)
  int distance = duration * 0.034 / 2;

  Serial.print("Distance: ");
  Serial.print(distance);
  Serial.println(" cm");

  delay(500);
}

Reacting to Distance 💡
Let’s add an LED to Pin 13 and turn it ON if an object gets closer than 10 cm!
void loop() {
  // ... (Distance Math code here) ...

  if (distance &lt; 10) {
    digitalWrite(9, HIGH); // Turn LED ON
  } else {
    digitalWrite(9, LOW);  // Turn LED OFF
  }
  delay(100); // Wait a bit before next reading
}

Reading the Position 👩‍💻
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Start the serial monitor
}

void loop() {
  int position = analogRead(A0); // Read the pin
  Serial.print("Dial Position: ");
  Serial.println(position);      // Will show a number from 0 to 1023
  delay(100);
}
This exact mechanism is hidden inside our next component…

Coding the Servo: Setup
Arduino has a built-in library for Servos! We don’t have to control the exact electrical pulses manually.
#include &lt;Servo.h&gt;  // Include the library

Servo myServo;  // Create a servo object

void setup() {
  myServo.attach(10); // Tell Arduino the servo is on pin 10
}

void loop() {
  // We'll add angles here
}

Pointing at Angles 📐
To make it point, we use myServo.write(angle). The angle is between 0 and 180.
void loop() {
  myServo.write(0);      // Turn all the way right
  delay(1000);           // Wait 1 second

  myServo.write(90);     // Turn to the middle
  delay(1000);

  myServo.write(180);    // Turn all the way left
  delay(1000);
}
Activity: Let’s wire this up and try making it point in 3 directions!

The “Sweep” 🧹
What if we want it to move smoothly from 0 to 180? We use a for loop to increase the angle slowly.
void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
  for (int pos = 0; pos &lt;= 180; pos += 1) { 
    myServo.write(pos);              
    delay(15); // Wait 15ms for servo to reach position                      
  }
}

The Radar Code Structure
We need to combine our two codes!

#include &lt;Servo.h&gt;
Setup Trigger, Echo, and Servo pins.
In loop(), command the servo to point to an angle (e.g., 30°), then pulse the sensor, and print the resulting distance!

Taking Readings in 3 Directions
